const BLOG = [
  { slug: "low-back-pain-hips", title: "Why your low back pain comes from your hips", published: "2026-11-03",
    excerpt: "In most adults with back pain, the spine is not the villain. The hips are. Here's how to tell, and what Dr. Jared actually treats.",
    h1: "Why your low back pain comes from your hips",
    sections: [
      { h2: "The myth: 'my back is weak'",
        body: "Most people with chronic low back pain assume their core is weak or their spine is damaged. Sometimes that's true. More often, the back is working harder than it should because the hips above and below it are not doing their share. The spine ends up as a shock absorber for a joint that was designed to move freely." },
      { h2: "What the hips are supposed to do",
        body: "Hips are big ball-and-socket joints with three jobs: let your leg move in all directions, drive power when you push off, and keep the pelvis level while you walk and stand. When the hip flexors in the front stay short from sitting, when the glutes in the back get sleepy from lack of use, and when the deep rotators stiffen from inactivity, the hip can't do those three jobs. The low back picks up the slack." },
      { h2: "Three signs your back pain is a hip problem",
        body: "First, your pain is worse after a long drive, a long flight or a long day at a desk, and it eases when you walk around. Sitting shortens the hip flexors, and standing up stretches them again. Second, you can't get into a deep squat with your feet flat without the low back flexing or your heels lifting. The hips are supposed to let you squat; the back shouldn't have to bend to make up for them. Third, when you stand on one leg, your hip drops to the other side. That's a glute medius problem on the standing leg." },
      { h2: "What we actually do about it",
        body: "In the clinic, the fix usually looks like this. First, hands-on soft-tissue work to release the iliopsoas (the deep hip flexor that runs from your spine down to your femur) and the lateral hip. Second, specific strengthening of the glutes with movements that teach them to fire when you walk, stand up and climb stairs. Third, mobility drills that open the hips without stressing the back. Fourth, a short daily home routine, usually 10–15 minutes, in the MD Fit app." },
      { h2: "What you can start today",
        body: "Try a 30-second couch stretch each side, a slow squat holding onto a doorframe for ten reps, and a side-lying clamshell for ten reps each side. These three alone, done every day for two weeks, change how a lot of backs feel." },
      { h2: "When back pain is not from the hips",
        body: "Red flags that need medical care: numbness in the groin, bowel or bladder changes, saddle numbness, or progressive weakness in a leg. If any of these are present, call your doctor or go to the ER. Everyday mechanical low back pain is different and almost always responds to the right rehab." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "three-muscles-knee-pain", title: "The 3 muscles behind most knee pain", published: "2026-10-27",
    excerpt: "Knee pain is almost never a knee problem. These three muscles above and below the joint do most of the damage, and most of the healing.",
    h1: "The 3 muscles behind most knee pain",
    sections: [
      { h2: "The knee is a messenger",
        body: "The knee is a simple hinge sandwiched between two complex joints: the hip above and the ankle below. When either of those joints has a problem, the knee is the first to complain. That's why so many people have knee pain that doesn't improve from treating the knee itself." },
      { h2: "Muscle 1: Vastus medialis (inner quad)",
        body: "The vastus medialis is the teardrop-shaped quad muscle on the inside of your knee. It helps track the kneecap. When it's weak, the kneecap drifts outward and grinds against the groove it's supposed to glide in. That's a huge chunk of patellofemoral pain, the generic 'runner's knee.' Fixing it looks like Spanish squats, terminal knee extensions and single-leg wall sits." },
      { h2: "Muscle 2: Gluteus medius (outer hip)",
        body: "The glute medius lives on the outside of your pelvis. Its job is to keep your pelvis level when you stand on one leg. When it's weak, the opposite hip drops, your femur rotates inward, and the knee caves. Every time you take a step, you grind the knee a little. Side-lying clamshells, side planks and single-leg bridges rebuild it. Many people with IT band pain are actually glute-weak." },
      { h2: "Muscle 3: Gastrocnemius and soleus (calves)",
        body: "The calves control how your lower leg loads over your foot. Tight calves limit how far your knee can travel over your toes. That means every squat, every stair, every landing from a jog puts more rotational force into the knee. Simple calf stretching helps a little; loaded calf raises help a lot, especially the slow-eccentric kind." },
      { h2: "Putting it together",
        body: "At MD Fit, the knee evaluation always includes the hip and the ankle. We find which of the three muscles above are letting you down, release them if they're tight, and strengthen them if they're weak. Clients typically do a short daily routine in the app and come in for hands-on work and progression." },
      { h2: "When to see your doctor",
        body: "Red flags: knee swelling that gets worse over hours, inability to bear weight, a knee that locks or gives way, or a fever with a hot knee. Any of those are reasons to call your doctor the same day." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "shoulder-clicks-when-to-worry", title: "Shoulder clicks: when to worry and when not to", published: "2026-10-20",
    excerpt: "A shoulder that clicks or pops is not always a problem. Here are the three questions that tell you whether to ignore it or take it seriously.",
    h1: "Shoulder clicks: when to worry and when not to",
    sections: [
      { h2: "Why shoulders click",
        body: "The shoulder is the loosest joint in the body. The ball of the humerus is bigger than the socket, which is why we can reach overhead but also why the shoulder can slip, pinch and click. Most clicks are harmless movements of a tendon over a bony edge." },
      { h2: "The three questions",
        body: "Question one: Does the click hurt? If you can click your shoulder on purpose and feel nothing, that is almost always fine. Painful clicks are the ones that need attention. Question two: Is it getting louder, more frequent, or weaker? A click that is stable for years is less concerning than a new click that is getting worse week to week. Question three: Can you still do what you need to? A click that doesn't limit lifting, reaching or sleep is usually safe to leave alone." },
      { h2: "Clicks that need a professional",
        body: "Pain with the click, especially in a specific position overhead. A feeling of catching or locking. Weakness, like dropping a glass or trouble reaching behind your back. Pain at night that wakes you up. New clicks after a fall or accident." },
      { h2: "What a clinic visit looks like",
        body: "At MD Fit, a shoulder evaluation checks shoulder-blade control, rotator cuff strength, neck involvement and how your shoulder tracks when you lift. Most clicky shoulders turn out to be scapular control problems, where the shoulder blade isn't moving smoothly with the arm. The fix is simple strengthening and some targeted mobility, usually 6–12 weeks." },
      { h2: "When it is an actual tear",
        body: "Some clicks come from labral tears or rotator cuff tears. Not all of these need surgery. Many can be managed well with rehab, especially in middle-aged and older adults where tendon changes are common. The evaluation tells you which bucket your shoulder is in." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "desk-worker-5-fixes", title: "Sitting all day? 5 fixes a desk worker can start today", published: "2026-10-13",
    excerpt: "You can't quit your job. But you can stop your body from paying the price of eight hours at a desk. Five changes you can start this afternoon.",
    h1: "Sitting all day? 5 fixes a desk worker can start today",
    sections: [
      { h2: "The problem isn't just posture",
        body: "The problem with desk work isn't that you sit badly. It's that you sit for too long, in the same position, with your head craned forward and your hip flexors shortened. The fix isn't a fancier chair. The fix is interrupting the pattern." },
      { h2: "Fix 1: The 30-minute rule",
        body: "Set a timer. Every 30 minutes, stand up, take ten steps, roll your shoulders back, and sit down. That one minute is enough to restart circulation in your legs, decompress your spine and remind your muscles they exist. If you do nothing else from this list, do this one." },
      { h2: "Fix 2: The chin tuck",
        body: "Three times a day, sit tall, pull your chin straight back (not up or down) toward your throat as if making a small double-chin, hold for five seconds, release. Ten reps. This counters the forward-head posture that drives tension headaches and neck pain." },
      { h2: "Fix 3: The couch stretch",
        body: "Pick a time (right before dinner, or during a show) and do a 30-second couch stretch on each side. Kneel in front of a couch or wall, put the top of your foot behind you against the couch, squeeze your glute and tuck your pelvis under. Eight hours of sitting shortens the hip flexors. One minute a day keeps them honest." },
      { h2: "Fix 4: The doorway pec stretch",
        body: "Stand in a doorway, hands on the frame at shoulder height, step one foot through, feel the stretch across the front of your chest and shoulders. 30 seconds. Rounded shoulders at a desk get tight pecs. This re-opens the chest and lets the shoulder blades sit flat." },
      { h2: "Fix 5: Walk after dinner",
        body: "A 15-minute walk after dinner is one of the highest-return things you can do for a desk-worker body. Blood sugar, hips, calves, mood, sleep: it helps all of them. If this feels obvious, it's because it's the one that works and no one does it." },
      { h2: "When to come in",
        body: "If you have persistent neck tension, headaches more than once a week, numbness in the hands, or back pain that doesn't ease on weekends, these five fixes aren't enough. Book an evaluation and we'll build you a specific plan." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "knee-replacement-12-week-comeback", title: "After knee replacement: a safe 12-week comeback", published: "2026-10-06",
    excerpt: "A total knee replacement is one of the most successful operations in medicine. Here's what the first 12 weeks should look like, week by week.",
    h1: "After knee replacement: a safe 12-week comeback",
    sections: [
      { h2: "Weeks 1–2: Control the swelling, protect the range",
        body: "The first two weeks are about swelling, pain and range of motion. Elevate the leg above your heart several times a day. Ice for 15 minutes every 2–3 hours. Walk short distances every hour. Follow the home-exercise plan your surgeon's team gave you. The goal is zero degrees of extension (knee fully straight) and about 90 degrees of flexion. These numbers matter: losing extension in the first weeks is hard to recover." },
      { h2: "Weeks 3–4: Build the walking base",
        body: "Transition off the walker to a cane, then to no device as your gait improves. Target 10–15 minutes of continuous walking. Start closed-chain strength: wall sits, mini squats to a chair, step-ups on a 4-inch step. Keep working on flexion. If you aren't at 110 degrees of flexion by week 4, we push harder." },
      { h2: "Weeks 5–8: Add real strength",
        body: "This is when the clinic work really matters. We add step-ups, leg press, single-leg balance, and gentle hip work. Walking distance moves to 30 minutes. You should be able to climb a flight of stairs normally. Many clients return to driving around week 5 or 6 depending on which leg was operated on and their surgeon's clearance." },
      { h2: "Weeks 9–12: Return to activity",
        body: "By week 9, most clients are walking normally, climbing stairs without a railing, and doing resistance training. Golfers restart short putting and chipping around week 8, work up to 9 holes by week 10, and full 18 by weeks 11–12. Pickleball players are usually back to light rallying around week 10, full matches by week 12 or 14. Swimming returns around week 6 once incisions are fully healed." },
      { h2: "The goal at 12 weeks",
        body: "Zero to 120+ degrees of knee flexion. Normal walking gait with no limp. Ability to climb a flight of stairs normally. Single-leg balance for 30 seconds. Ten controlled step-ups. Return to the recreational activities you did before, within reason." },
      { h2: "Common mistakes",
        body: "Trying to walk too far too soon, which flares swelling and sets you back. Skipping the home program on the days you aren't in the clinic. Chasing flexion without protecting extension. Returning to high-impact activity before the strength is there. We help you avoid all of these." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "pickleball-injuries-sarasota", title: "Pickleball injuries we see most in Sarasota", published: "2026-09-29",
    excerpt: "Pickleball is the fastest-growing sport in Sarasota. Here are the five injuries we see most in the clinic and how to avoid becoming one of them.",
    h1: "Pickleball injuries we see most in Sarasota",
    sections: [
      { h2: "Why pickleball injuries are so common",
        body: "Pickleball looks easy. It isn't. The quick starts and stops, the lateral shuffles, the overhead smashes and the paddle grip all stress the body in specific ways. Add that most new players are over 50 and haven't done athletic movement in years, and you have a predictable list of injuries." },
      { h2: "Injury 1: Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow)",
        body: "Pain on the outside of the paddle-side elbow, worse with gripping. Caused by the paddle hand doing too much work. Fix: lighter paddle grip, better technique, and a specific loading program for the wrist extensors. Treatment is usually 4–8 weeks." },
      { h2: "Injury 2: Rotator cuff pain",
        body: "Especially from overhead smashes and backhand drives in players who haven't done overhead work in years. Fix: scapular control first, cuff strength second, then gradually loading overhead patterns. Protocol is usually 6 weeks." },
      { h2: "Injury 3: Calf strains",
        body: "The classic 'pickleball calf.' You lunge for a drop shot, feel a pop in the calf, and you're done for the day. Fix: a graded return-to-court plan with calf raises, isometric holds and eccentric work. Most strains heal fully in 4–6 weeks with the right loading." },
      { h2: "Injury 4: Low back pain",
        body: "Especially from the kneeling-into-stand reach for low volleys and from twisting at the back instead of the hips. Fix: hip mobility, glute strength and teaching the player to rotate from the hips, not the lumbar spine." },
      { h2: "Injury 5: Achilles tendon pain",
        body: "The second-most-common lower-leg injury. Stiff, sore Achilles that is worse first thing in the morning. Fix: calf-strengthening program (slow heel raises off a step), court-time management while the tendon rebuilds, and often a shoe change." },
      { h2: "How to stay on the court",
        body: "Three keys. One: do a real warm-up (not just 'rally for a few minutes'). Our 10-minute pickleball warm-up is in the MD Fit app. Two: strength-train twice a week. Even light strength work is protective. Three: respect the first signal. The injuries above almost always start with a small ache a week or two before the day you can't play. Fix small things small." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "running-over-50", title: "Running without pain over 50", published: "2026-09-22",
    excerpt: "You can keep running past 50, 60 and beyond. You just can't train like you're 25. Here's what changes.",
    h1: "Running without pain over 50",
    sections: [
      { h2: "The body does change",
        body: "Three things change for runners over 50. Tendons stiffen and need longer to adapt to new loads. Recovery takes longer, especially between hard sessions. Muscle mass drops about 1% a year starting around 40 unless you actively fight it. None of these means you have to stop running. All of them mean the plan has to change." },
      { h2: "Change 1: Add strength training",
        body: "If you are not strength training by age 50, your running career has a timer on it. Two sessions a week of basic lower-body strength (squats, hinges, split squats, calf raises) protects your tendons, muscles and bones. Clients who add strength training usually say their running feels easier within 4–6 weeks." },
      { h2: "Change 2: Protect sleep and recovery",
        body: "Most runners over 50 can handle three to four quality running days a week. Four to five hard sessions a week usually leads to injury within a few months. Make the easy days easy, the hard days hard, and give yourself the sleep to adapt." },
      { h2: "Change 3: Think in weeks, not days",
        body: "Younger runners can push a hard session the day after a hard session. Over 50, think about load across the whole week. The question isn't 'how do I feel today?' It's 'how is this week landing on top of last week?'" },
      { h2: "The specific injuries we see",
        body: "Achilles tendon pain, plantar fasciitis, hamstring tendon pain near the sit bone, and knee pain are the big four. All of them respond well to targeted loading programs that use the real research on tendons: specific, progressive, and more loading, not less. If you have one of these and have been resting for weeks, call us. The fix is probably not more rest." },
      { h2: "What to do if you want to keep going",
        body: "Book an evaluation. We assess your running gait, your strength, your mobility and your training history. You leave with a plan that lets you keep running without the next flare." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "sciatica-or-glute", title: "Sciatica or glute pain? How to tell", published: "2026-09-15",
    excerpt: "They can feel the same. The treatment is very different. Three questions help you tell which one you have.",
    h1: "Sciatica or glute pain? How to tell",
    sections: [
      { h2: "Why they get confused",
        body: "True sciatica is irritation of the sciatic nerve, usually from a disc or a tight muscle pressing on it. Glute pain is muscle pain in the buttock. Both can shoot pain into the back of the leg. Both are worse with sitting. Both can be miserable. The treatments are very different." },
      { h2: "Question 1: How far does the pain go?",
        body: "True sciatica typically shoots all the way past the knee, often into the calf or foot, and may follow a specific path along the back or outside of the leg. Glute pain is usually in the buttock and upper thigh, and doesn't travel below the knee." },
      { h2: "Question 2: Are there numbness or tingling?",
        body: "True sciatica often includes numbness, pins and needles, or weakness along the leg or foot. Glute pain is deep and achy but usually doesn't come with those neurological symptoms." },
      { h2: "Question 3: What makes it better and worse?",
        body: "Sciatica is often worse with certain back positions (bending forward or backward, depending on the cause) and may respond to specific nerve-glide exercises. Glute pain is often worse with pressure on the buttock (long drives, sitting on a hard chair) and responds to muscle release and strengthening." },
      { h2: "Red flags that mean call your doctor now",
        body: "Loss of bowel or bladder control. Numbness in the groin or saddle area. Rapidly progressive weakness in a leg. Any of these are emergency-care red flags, no matter which of the two conditions you think you have." },
      { h2: "What we do",
        body: "The evaluation separates the two in the first visit. Most true sciatica responds to rehabilitation without surgery when the right loading is prescribed. Most glute pain responds to targeted soft-tissue work and glute strengthening. Both get a daily home routine in the MD Fit app." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "golf-back-pain-hip-problem", title: "Golf back pain: it's usually a hip problem", published: "2026-09-08",
    excerpt: "You can swing the club another 20 years. You just have to let your hips do their job.",
    h1: "Golf back pain: it's usually a hip problem",
    sections: [
      { h2: "The golf swing in two sentences",
        body: "The golf swing is a rotational power move. The hips are supposed to turn, the thoracic spine (upper back) is supposed to turn, and the low back is supposed to stay relatively neutral. When hips or thoracic spine don't turn, the low back turns for them, and the low back wasn't built for that." },
      { h2: "The two hip problems",
        body: "Problem one: not enough internal rotation in the trail hip (the right hip for a right-handed golfer). You can't finish your backswing without the back having to twist. Problem two: not enough glute strength on the lead side. You can't rotate powerfully through impact without the back snapping into extension." },
      { h2: "The thoracic spine piece",
        body: "If your mid-back is stiff from sitting at a desk all day, the shoulder turn has to come from the low back. We check thoracic rotation on every golfer. A quick test: sit tall in a chair, cross your arms over your chest, turn as far as you can each way. If one side is noticeably worse than the other, that's the side where the back is doing the work for the mid-back." },
      { h2: "What we actually fix",
        body: "Hands-on soft-tissue work to open up hip rotation. Targeted thoracic mobility drills (open books, seated rotations, cat-cow with reach). Specific glute strengthening (single-leg deadlifts, cable rotations, step-up variations). A short daily routine in the MD Fit app you can do before golf and on off days." },
      { h2: "What to expect",
        body: "Most golfers notice their swing feels easier within 3–4 weeks of consistent work. Driving distance often improves slightly because the lead hip can rotate through impact. More importantly, the low back pain that was happening by hole 12 starts happening less often, then stops." },
      { h2: "Should I keep playing while we work?",
        body: "Usually yes, with changes. Fewer holes, a shorter range session, and a specific warm-up. We give you clear limits each week so you can stay on the course while the hips and back rebuild." }
    ], faqs: [] },

  { slug: "what-is-a-cep", title: "What is a Clinical Exercise Physiologist (CEP)?", published: "2026-09-01",
    excerpt: "You know what a physical therapist is. You know what a personal trainer is. Here's what a Clinical Exercise Physiologist does and why Dr. Jared is one.",
    h1: "What is a Clinical Exercise Physiologist (CEP)?",
    sections: [
      { h2: "Where CEPs come from",
        body: "A Clinical Exercise Physiologist (CEP) is a credentialed exercise clinician who specializes in using exercise to prevent and manage chronic disease. The credential is issued through the American College of Sports Medicine (ACSM). The scope of practice includes cardiovascular, metabolic, pulmonary, neurological, orthopedic and some oncologic conditions." },
      { h2: "What a CEP actually does",
        body: "A CEP designs and delivers exercise programs for people whose medical history makes exercise more complex than 'go to the gym and figure it out.' That includes people with heart disease, high blood pressure, diabetes, obesity, osteoporosis, Parkinson's disease, multiple sclerosis, cancer recovery, long COVID, and more. CEPs can also prescribe exercise for healthy adults and athletes; the extra training just means they can safely handle the complex cases." },
      { h2: "CEP vs. CCEP",
        body: "CEP is the base credential. CCEP (Certified Clinical Exercise Physiologist, through ACSM) is the advanced credential, with higher training requirements and a longer board exam. Dr. Jared holds both." },
      { h2: "CEP vs. Physical Therapist",
        body: "Physical therapists work after an injury or surgery, usually in insurance-reimbursed care, focused on recovering function. CEPs often work before or alongside PT, focused on exercise prescription for people with medical conditions, and can continue past the point PT ends to build long-term strength and conditioning. The two professions overlap, and the best outcomes often come from both working together." },
      { h2: "CEP vs. Personal Trainer",
        body: "A personal trainer is a fitness professional, no medical scope. A CEP is a healthcare clinician. If you have a diagnosis of any kind, especially cardiovascular or metabolic, a CEP is the right starting point. If you are healthy and want to get fitter, both can help." },
      { h2: "Why this matters at MD Fit",
        body: "Dr. Jared is both a physician and a CEP, so the clinic can care for people with complex medical histories in the same place where they get their rehabilitation and strength work. Nothing has to be coordinated between three different providers. For people in Sarasota, Bradenton, Lakewood Ranch, Venice and Osprey with a diagnosis who want to get stronger safely, that integration is the whole point." }
    ], faqs: [] }
];

